WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.368
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy

00:00:02.368 --> 00:00:04.637
and Tony Award-winning actor
and one of the stars

00:00:04.637 --> 00:00:06.672
of "Dicks: The Musical."

00:00:06.672 --> 00:00:08.108
The soundtrack is available now

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:10.610
and the film opens nationwide
this Friday.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:11.978
Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:15.548
our very good friend
Nathan Lane, everybody.

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:17.884
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:33.565 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:33.565 --> 00:00:35.635
Welcome back, Nathan.

00:00:35.635 --> 00:00:38.237
-Oh, it's nice to be back.
Nice to see you.

00:00:38.237 --> 00:00:39.806
-You're back
to your snazzy self.

00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:41.975
You're wearing a pocket square.
You look like $1 million.

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:45.478
-Oh, thank you.
Thank you very much.

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:47.347
You, too, as always.

00:00:47.347 --> 00:00:51.016
[ Laughter ]

00:00:51.016 --> 00:00:53.720
-You were here right
before the writers strike.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.555
And so we've been apart
for a little over five months.

00:00:56.555 --> 00:00:58.024
-I know, I know.
-Did you use the time well?

00:00:58.024 --> 00:01:00.160
-Well, you know,
I'm very happy

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.594
the writers strike
resolved favorably

00:01:01.594 --> 00:01:02.762
for the writers, of course.

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:05.765
And more importantly,
that it brought you back

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:08.000
to late night where you belong.
-Oh, thank you.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.303
-I --
Oh, there was one clap.

00:01:10.303 --> 00:01:13.639
[ Laughter ]

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:14.774
Wow.

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:17.142
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:17.142 --> 00:01:18.444
Because...

00:01:18.444 --> 00:01:21.881
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:23.716
Because quite -- quite honestly,

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:25.151
I didn't know
what to do with myself

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:27.487
when you weren't doing --
doing the show.

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:31.490
No, no, I was getting
far too much sleep.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:34.894
I became downright perky,
and no one wants to see that.

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:38.164
I was so perky I had to rewatch
"The Crown" just to stabilize.

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:39.398
[ Laughter ]

00:01:39.398 --> 00:01:40.900
-Oh, to bring it back down?
-Yeah.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.070
Without you, the only thing
keeping me up at night

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.806
was flashbacks
of the Lauren Boebert video,

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:49.475
where she was vaping
and groping

00:01:49.475 --> 00:01:51.911
her boyfriend during
"Beetlejuice: The Musical."

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:53.846
-Yes, of course,
-Perhaps Republicans

00:01:53.846 --> 00:01:56.849
should just avoid
theater altogether.

00:01:56.849 --> 00:01:59.852
I'm sure Lincoln would have
agreed with that.

00:01:59.852 --> 00:02:01.621
Nevertheless --
Oh, too soon?

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:06.793
Nevertheless, I'm sure I'm not
the only "Late Night" viewer

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:08.795
who built a tiny replica
of your desk

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:12.431
and put their dog behind it
dressed like you.

00:02:12.431 --> 00:02:14.266
It wasn't quite the same,

00:02:14.266 --> 00:02:17.803
but I did enjoy his segment
called "A Closer Woof."

00:02:17.803 --> 00:02:19.472
-Oh,

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:21.574
-That was a joke
just for Frisbee.

00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:23.042
-Oh, my dog?
-Just for your dog, Frisbee.

00:02:23.042 --> 00:02:24.777
-My dog will watch tonight
for the first time

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:27.113
and she'll get one of the jokes.
-That's right.

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:28.614
-The Lauren Boebert thing
must have been

00:02:28.614 --> 00:02:29.649
a little harrowing for you

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:31.684
being a longtime
Broadway performer.

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:33.453
Have you ever seen
that sort of --

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:35.387
that sort of beeswax
going on?

00:02:35.387 --> 00:02:36.956
-In the theater?
-Yeah.

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:38.991
-Well -- well, sure.

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:43.495
I mean, you know, there --
No, it's a free for all now.

00:02:43.495 --> 00:02:47.066
No, you know, but speaking
of free for alls,

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:50.803
I just went to see the Madonna
Celebration Tour in London.

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:52.305
It was astonishing.

00:02:52.305 --> 00:02:54.240
You know, we're approximately
the same age.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.475
-You and Madonna
-And I find -- me and Madonna.

00:02:56.475 --> 00:02:59.112
-Yeah.
-And what kind of a gay man

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:02.147
would I be if I didn't go to
the Madonna Celebration tour?

00:03:02.147 --> 00:03:04.050
-It's like a celebration
of you too in a way.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.318
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:03:05.318 --> 00:03:08.588
And I just find her
inspiring on so many levels.

00:03:08.588 --> 00:03:10.956
And while I was watching,
I said to myself,

00:03:10.956 --> 00:03:13.892
"I need to get a pair
of fishnet compression socks."

00:03:13.892 --> 00:03:15.995
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:03:15.995 --> 00:03:17.196
But I did think, you know,

00:03:17.196 --> 00:03:19.765
it must be tough
to be a rocker at 65

00:03:19.765 --> 00:03:22.335
when the only white powder
backstage is Gold Bond.

00:03:22.335 --> 00:03:24.704
[ Laughter ]

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:30.476
Too much voguing can cause
carpal tunnel syndrome.

00:03:30.476 --> 00:03:32.245
-That's true.
-But there she was,

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:34.847
acting like a virgin
touched for the very first time.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:36.882
-Oh, good for her.
-It was very moving.

00:03:36.882 --> 00:03:38.685
-Very moving.
Now, you know,

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:40.853
you talk about the fact
that you're almost

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:42.187
the same age as her,
and that means

00:03:42.187 --> 00:03:43.222
you've had
a very long career.

00:03:43.222 --> 00:03:44.323
We obviously know that.

00:03:44.323 --> 00:03:46.292
Have you been approached
to write a memoir?

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.027
-Yeah, I'm trying, I'm trying.
-Oh, you're trying right now?

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:49.962
-It's very difficult.
Yeah, yeah.

00:03:49.962 --> 00:03:52.198
The hardest parts are
the remembering...

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:54.067
[ Laughter ]

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.402
...and the writing.
-Yeah.

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:59.204
-And -- and, oh
and the remembering.

00:03:59.204 --> 00:04:00.873
-[ Laughs ]
-You know, I should have --

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:03.042
I should have written things
down along the way.

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:04.711
I should have kept a journal,
but I didn't.

00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:06.279
It's like going into
a grocery store

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:07.846
and realizing
I should have written a list

00:04:07.846 --> 00:04:09.949
for what I came here for.

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:11.550
Instead of forgetting bananas,

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.254
I'm forgetting my 20s,
30s and 40s.

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:18.791
Cosmopolitans may have played
a major factor in that,

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:20.893
but I don't have
many bombshells

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:24.497
like -- like Jada Pinkett Smith
revealing she --

00:04:24.497 --> 00:04:26.431
she and Will Smith have been

00:04:26.431 --> 00:04:28.568
secretly separated
for seven years.

00:04:28.568 --> 00:04:29.769
W.T.F.

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:31.136
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.136 --> 00:04:33.872
As the kids would say,
W.T.F.!

00:04:33.872 --> 00:04:36.975
-Oh, wow. You're doing it in a
very believable kids way.

00:04:36.975 --> 00:04:38.411
-Aren't I?

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:41.414
You know, and still
they -- they work together,

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:43.782
they raised the children, they
attended events as a couple.

00:04:43.782 --> 00:04:49.122
It seems like maybe
it was a secret even from them.

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:50.723
-Oh, you don't think
they even knew?

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:52.692
-I started to wonder
maybe my husband and I

00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:54.827
are secretly separated here.

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:57.697
All this time I just thought
he was in the other room

00:04:57.697 --> 00:04:59.999
watching bad reality shows.

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:01.434
I guess I won't figure it out

00:05:01.434 --> 00:05:04.270
until he gets up and slaps
someone at the Tony Awards.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.439
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.673 --> 00:05:09.274
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:09.274 --> 00:05:11.077
-Oh, real quick.
-Yeah.

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:13.012
-Real quick, and then we're
going to go to commercial.

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:14.880
But, you know,
speaking of the Tonys,

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.082
you and I are working
on a -- on a musical.

00:05:17.082 --> 00:05:18.751
-Oh, geez.
-"Cicada, Cicada."

00:05:18.751 --> 00:05:20.219
-Oh, well, that's the elephant
in the room.

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:21.520
-It is the elephant.
-The question on

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.490
everybody's minds is
"Cicada, Cicada: The Musical."

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.259
Now, I'm not --
I'm not privy to everything,

00:05:27.259 --> 00:05:28.795
but I've heard that the script

00:05:28.795 --> 00:05:30.263
and the score
are still in development.

00:05:30.263 --> 00:05:31.363
-Yes.
-And it's not ready

00:05:31.363 --> 00:05:32.765
for a table read.
-No.

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:35.267
-But I'm pleased to announce
we have picked out a table.

00:05:35.267 --> 00:05:37.403
-[ Laughing ] We have.

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:41.407
-And the good news is the title
is testing through the roof

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:43.709
with both entomologists
and entomologists.

00:05:43.709 --> 00:05:45.778
-Oh, that's good.

00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:49.748
-[ Laughing ] Because Broadway
has not taken advantage

00:05:49.748 --> 00:05:53.552
of the insect wordplay market

00:05:53.552 --> 00:05:55.554
since "Spider-Man:
Turn Off the Dark."

00:05:55.554 --> 00:05:57.023
-That's true.
-We're on to something.

00:05:57.023 --> 00:05:59.125
-We're on to that.
-But we got to act fast

00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:01.994
because I don't want
some copycat to poach us.

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:05.364
And suddenly there's two
cicada-themed musicals

00:06:05.364 --> 00:06:06.632
vying for Broadway.

00:06:06.632 --> 00:06:08.067
-Alright, well,
we're going to get --

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:09.769
we're going to try to get
an actual song

00:06:09.769 --> 00:06:11.603
from "Cicada, Cicada"...
-Yes.

00:06:11.603 --> 00:06:13.673
-...on this show.
The act one finale.

00:06:13.673 --> 00:06:17.543
-The act one finale
within the next six months?

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:20.279
-Christine Baranski,
Harvey Fierstein and the Mets.

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:22.648
-Yes, we're going to get
most of the Mets.

00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:24.250
-Most of the Mets.
-Yeah.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.184
You know, the Mets are
very excited about this.

00:06:26.184 --> 00:06:27.986
-I bet.
-It's when you have --

00:06:27.986 --> 00:06:29.655
when you don't have
postseason baseball

00:06:29.655 --> 00:06:31.724
to look forward to,
you look forward to the stage.

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:33.292
[ Laughter ]

00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:35.961
I have so much more to ask you
about "Dicks: The Musical,"

00:06:35.961 --> 00:06:37.129
which is a real thing.

00:06:37.129 --> 00:06:39.132
We'll be right back
with more Nathan Lane.

